{"author":"John Wesley","book":"Notes on the Bible","chapter":953,"classification":{"bucket":"Christian Classics / Commentaries","canonicalStatus":"noncanonical","contentKind":"commentary","cautionReason":"","doctrinalStatus":"historical","labels":["christian-classics","commentaries"],"severity":0},"id":"classics:wesley:Isa.15","lineage":[],"metadata":{"author":"John Wesley","authorshipDateRange":"18th century","genre":"Commentaries","tradition":"Methodist","translator":null,"edition":"CCEL edition; source omits 2 Samuel 24","rights":"Public Domain","source":{"id":"wesley","provider":"CCEL","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/wesley/notes.html","title":"Notes on the Bible"}},"originalBook":"Isaiah","sourceVolumeId":"wesley","source":{"id":"wesley","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/wesley/notes.html","title":"Notes on the Bible"},"title":"Isaiah 15","reference":"Isaiah 15","notes":[],"verses":[{"verse":1,"text":"The Destruction of Moab, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":2,"text":"1-9.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":3,"text":"1.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":4,"text":"The burden - A prophecy of the destruction of the Moabites, the inveterate enemies of the Jews, begun by the Assyrian, and finished by the Babylonian emperors.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":5,"text":"In a night - Suddenly and unexpectedly.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":6,"text":"Ar - The chief city of Moab.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":7,"text":"Kir - Another eminent city of Moab.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":8,"text":"2.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":9,"text":"Bajith - Which signifies an house.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":10,"text":"It is supposed to be some eminent house or temple of their idols.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":11,"text":"Dibon - Another city of Moab.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":12,"text":"To weep - To offer their supplications with tears to their idols for help.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":13,"text":"Medeba - Two considerable cities, anciently belonging to the Moabites.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":14,"text":"Beard - The hair of their heads and beards was shaved, as was usual in great mournings.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":15,"text":"3.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":3,"scriptureVerseEnd":3},{"verse":16,"text":"On the tops - Which were made flat, to which men used to go up, to cry to God in heaven, or to men for help.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":3,"scriptureVerseEnd":3},{"verse":17,"text":"4.","sourceParagraph":5,"scriptureVerseStart":4,"scriptureVerseEnd":4},{"verse":18,"text":"Heshbon - Two other Moabitish cities.","sourceParagraph":5,"scriptureVerseStart":4,"scriptureVerseEnd":4},{"verse":19,"text":"Jahaz - Another city in the utmost borders of Moab.","sourceParagraph":5,"scriptureVerseStart":4,"scriptureVerseEnd":4},{"verse":20,"text":"Soldiers - Who use to be the most courageous.","sourceParagraph":5,"scriptureVerseStart":4,"scriptureVerseEnd":4},{"verse":21,"text":"5.","sourceParagraph":6,"scriptureVerseStart":5,"scriptureVerseEnd":5},{"verse":22,"text":"Moab - Tho' they are a most vile nation.","sourceParagraph":6,"scriptureVerseStart":5,"scriptureVerseEnd":5},{"verse":23,"text":"Zoar - Zoar was a town bordering upon Moab.","sourceParagraph":6,"scriptureVerseStart":5,"scriptureVerseEnd":5},{"verse":24,"text":"Of destruction - Such a cry as men send forth when they are just falling into the pit of destruction.","sourceParagraph":6,"scriptureVerseStart":5,"scriptureVerseEnd":5},{"verse":25,"text":"6.","sourceParagraph":7,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6},{"verse":26,"text":"Waters - Watery grounds being very fruitful, are commonly most inhabited; but now they also, much more the dry and barren grounds, shall be desolate and without inhabitant.","sourceParagraph":7,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6},{"verse":27,"text":"7.","sourceParagraph":8,"scriptureVerseStart":7,"scriptureVerseEnd":7},{"verse":28,"text":"They - Their enemies.","sourceParagraph":8,"scriptureVerseStart":7,"scriptureVerseEnd":7},{"verse":29,"text":"Brook - Possibly he means some such river which ran into Euphrates, and so gave them opportunity of carrying their spoils by water unto Babylon.","sourceParagraph":8,"scriptureVerseStart":7,"scriptureVerseEnd":7},{"verse":30,"text":"8.","sourceParagraph":9,"scriptureVerseStart":8,"scriptureVerseEnd":8},{"verse":31,"text":"The cry - Their cry fills all the parts of the country.","sourceParagraph":9,"scriptureVerseStart":8,"scriptureVerseEnd":8},{"verse":32,"text":"9.","sourceParagraph":10,"scriptureVerseStart":9,"scriptureVerseEnd":9},{"verse":33,"text":"More - More than hath been already mentioned.","sourceParagraph":10,"scriptureVerseStart":9,"scriptureVerseEnd":9},{"verse":34,"text":"Lions - God shall send lions to find out those that escape the fury of men.","sourceParagraph":10,"scriptureVerseStart":9,"scriptureVerseEnd":9}],"scriptureReference":{"book":"Isaiah","chapter":15}}